WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.037 --> 00:00:03.241
In the middle of the 19th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.241 --> 00:00:10.580
a scientist stared into his microscope&nbsp;
and found, staring back at him, a vampire.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:15.449
Like the creatures of legend, the&nbsp;
organism he saw was a shape-shifter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.449 --> 00:00:20.236
though not the kind whose body contorts&nbsp;
from something vaguely human

00:00:20.236 --> 00:00:22.723
into something more like a bat.

00:00:22.723 --> 00:00:25.813
No, what he found was an amoeba.

00:00:25.813 --> 00:00:30.738
And it was in search of&nbsp;
something to sink its fangs into.

00:00:46.231 --> 00:00:52.463
The amoebas we’re discussing today belong to the&nbsp;
order Vampyrellida, which holds two families.

00:00:52.463 --> 00:00:59.191
The first is the family Vampyrellidae, made&nbsp;
up of organisms known generally as vampyrella.

00:00:59.191 --> 00:01:05.411
And the second Family is Leptophryidae,&nbsp;
which holds essentially everything else.

00:01:05.411 --> 00:01:09.908
The Leptophrys we’re looking at now, the&nbsp;
Arachnula we opened the episode with,

00:01:09.908 --> 00:01:11.612
and a bunch others.

00:01:11.612 --> 00:01:16.368
Now we’re not here today to navigate&nbsp;the
complicated taxonomic histories here though.

00:01:16.368 --> 00:01:19.636
We want to state upfront&nbsp;
that while we’re going to be using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.636 --> 00:01:22.578
the name “vampyrellids” as a convenient label,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.578 --> 00:01:29.069
the organisms who are gathered under that&nbsp;
umbrella have a wider array of identities.

00:01:29.069 --> 00:01:33.528
“Vampyrella” translates to&nbsp;
“little vampire” in Latin.

00:01:33.528 --> 00:01:37.740
The name was first given to members&nbsp;
of the Vampyrellida order in 1865&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:41.580
by the Polish microbiologist Leon Cienkowski,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:46.937
who was very much inspired by the vampires&nbsp;
of lore when naming these creatures.

00:01:46.937 --> 00:01:51.994
When you think of vampires, perhaps&nbsp;
you think of blood and viciousness.

00:01:51.994 --> 00:01:54.878
But the vampyrellid does not want blood.

00:01:54.878 --> 00:01:56.940
It wants is cytoplasm.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:01.560
And yes, it will be vicious to get what it wants.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:07.075
We know of about 50 species of vampyrellids,&nbsp;
scattered all around the world.

00:02:07.075 --> 00:02:13.080
Some, like ours, live in ponds, living&nbsp;
among filamentous algae and desmids.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.533
Others prefer marine environments,&nbsp;
while some can be found in soil.

00:02:17.533 --> 00:02:19.860
But despite this range of environments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:25.401
they share a very distinct life&nbsp;
cycle that revolves around feeding.

00:02:25.401 --> 00:02:28.565
The first stage of life is the trophozoite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.565 --> 00:02:32.283
though in the past it’s also&nbsp;
been known as the swarmer stage.

00:02:32.283 --> 00:02:37.263
That’s because this is the time of the&nbsp;
vampyrellid’s life when it is on the move,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.263 --> 00:02:41.320
or perhaps more accurately,&nbsp;
when it is on the hunt—

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:45.924
moving with one purpose: to find food.

00:02:45.924 --> 00:02:52.172
Luckily for them (but not for their prey),&nbsp;
vampyrellids have an expansive palate.

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:58.879
They’ve been known to consume everything from&nbsp;
green algae to diatoms to yeast to rotifer eggs.

00:02:58.879 --> 00:03:03.480
With such a wide menu to choose from,&nbsp;
vampyrellids wield a number of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:08.384
strategies to capture their prey, each of&nbsp;
which manages to sound a little bit

00:03:08.384 --> 00:03:11.137
creepier than the last.

00:03:11.137 --> 00:03:14.595
The least unsettling of all is free capture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.595 --> 00:03:18.052
a method of eating that’s quite&nbsp;
common throughout the microcosmos.

00:03:18.052 --> 00:03:21.753
The vampyrellid catches their food&nbsp;
and then swallows them up in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.753 --> 00:03:26.645
food vacuole that can break down the
prey to extract the nutrients they need.

00:03:26.645 --> 00:03:29.700
It doesn't sound like a pleasant&nbsp;
thing to go through as prey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:33.047
but it’s a method of eating that is
at least familiar.

00:03:33.047 --> 00:03:38.155
The next method though is a little more&nbsp;
disconcerting, starting with the name:

00:03:38.155 --> 00:03:40.336
colony invasion.

00:03:40.336 --> 00:03:44.580
With this strategy, the vampyrellid&nbsp;
finds its way into algae colonies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:52.383
digging past the gelatinous matrix that holds them&nbsp;
together so that it can be surrounded by its prey.

00:03:52.383 --> 00:03:58.860
Not only does the vampyrellid effectively take&nbsp;
up residence in a buffet, it is able to hide out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:04.900
in that colony as well, staying out of sight from&nbsp;
other predators that might want to come after it.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:10.800
It turns its prey into a shield&nbsp;
and restaurant all at once.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.601
Vampyrellids can also consume&nbsp;
their prey through something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.601 --> 00:04:19.660
called protoplast extraction, which perhaps&nbsp;
sounds more mundane than what it actually is.

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:23.434
This is the feeding strategy&nbsp;
behind the vampyrellid’s name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.434 --> 00:04:28.805
the action that Cienkowski and plenty of other&nbsp;
scientists have witnessed and documented.

00:04:28.805 --> 00:04:32.520
Despite the impact of this method&nbsp;
on the scientific imagination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.429
we don’t actually know fully how it works.

00:04:36.429 --> 00:04:39.735
It's controlled by the vampyrellid’s pseudopodia—

00:04:39.735 --> 00:04:43.234
these long filaments that&nbsp;
branch out from its body.

00:04:43.234 --> 00:04:48.960
And the vampyrellid begins by poking&nbsp;
or dissolving holes in their prey.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:56.220
From there, they then use two feeding pseudopodia&nbsp;
to dig into the prey cell to act like straws…

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:59.682
or, you might say, like vampire fangs.

00:04:59.682 --> 00:05:04.620
And there’s actually one last vampyrellid 
approach&nbsp;to dining, though it’s quite rare.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:09.420
It’s called prey infiltration, and it’s&nbsp;
kind of like getting to the fourth entry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:13.435
in a horror movie series and realizing&nbsp;
every frightening image you’re about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.435 --> 00:05:19.897
to see is actually like a remix of previous&nbsp;
terrors taken to their most gruesome end.

00:05:19.897 --> 00:05:24.240
Prey infiltration begins similarly&nbsp;
to protoplast extraction:

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.098
with the vampyrellid puncturing its prey.

00:05:27.098 --> 00:05:32.760
But then it shifts into something more like colony&nbsp;
invasion, except instead of invading the colony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:39.581
the vampyrellid invades the body&nbsp;
of its prey to feed from within.

00:05:39.581 --> 00:05:45.120
Imagine Dracula not just sucking blood from&nbsp;
your neck, but somehow invading your body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:50.247
through the holes left by his fangs and&nbsp;
then consuming your blood from within.

00:05:50.247 --> 00:05:52.320
Or maybe don’t imagine it.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:56.135
Perhaps we’ve taken the 
metaphor a little too far.

00:05:56.135 --> 00:06:01.200
However the vampyrellid chooses to eat,&nbsp;
the next stage of its life is the same.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:08.340
It will turn from trophozoite to cyst, retracting&nbsp;
those long filamentous extensions inwards and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:14.015
forming a cell wall around itself that might&nbsp;
even attach to surfaces around the organism.

00:06:14.015 --> 00:06:16.920
We’ve seen plenty of cysts in the microcosmos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:20.914
each of which iterates on some&nbsp;
version of the same theory:

00:06:20.914 --> 00:06:25.170
when an organism finds itself in&nbsp;
dire environmental circumstances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:30.120
it holes up in a cyst where it&nbsp;
winds down its metabolism and rests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:35.004
waiting for things to clear up&nbsp;
so that it can emerge and survive.

00:06:35.004 --> 00:06:39.121
And while vampyrellids can&nbsp;
form that kind of resting cyst,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.121 --> 00:06:42.897
what we’re watching now is a&nbsp;
very different kind of state:

00:06:42.897 --> 00:06:46.861
this is a vampyrellid in a digestive cyst.

00:06:46.861 --> 00:06:48.915
It hasn’t shut off its metabolism.

00:06:48.915 --> 00:06:49.800
Quite the opposite.

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:56.040
The whole point of the digestive cyst is to give&nbsp;
the vampyrellid time to break down its meals.

00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:01.080
Some species will even change color,&nbsp;
turning red or yellow or some other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:07.080
color as its body responds to the changes&nbsp;
brought on by the vampyrellid’s meal.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:11.040
If we were to turn back to&nbsp;
popular images of vampires,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:17.760
perhaps this is the vampyrellid’s coffin, a&nbsp;
place for it to rest while it processes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:22.353
cytoplasmic sips it took from&nbsp;
the body of another organism.

00:07:22.353 --> 00:07:25.380
Except even then, the stories we tell of vampires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:30.243
fail to capture all of the twisted&nbsp;
tangles of the vampyrellid’s life.

00:07:30.243 --> 00:07:34.979
Because when the vampyrellid is&nbsp;
done digesting, it does two things.

00:07:34.979 --> 00:07:39.500
First it emerges from its cyst,&nbsp;
punching holes in the walls it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:44.515
used to encase itself and cycling&nbsp;
back into its trophozoite state.

00:07:44.515 --> 00:07:51.000
But sometimes more than one trophozoite emerges,&nbsp;
because while the vampyrellid was in the cyst,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:56.147
it might have divided once,&nbsp;
twice, maybe even more times.

00:07:56.147 --> 00:08:01.260
And second, the vampyrellid gets&nbsp;
rid of its undigested food remains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:07.456
leaving behind what scientists have&nbsp;
described as “numerous brown particles.”

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:12.720
So again, if we were to try&nbsp;
to put this in vampire terms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:15.660
imagine a vampire ready to&nbsp;
emerge from its coffin at night.

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:18.360
It’s digested its meals, it is ready to hunt.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:21.705
But first, the vampire needs&nbsp;
to clone itself a few times.

00:08:21.705 --> 00:08:23.640
And then, with its bloodthirsty clones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:28.192
our vampire will proceed to punch holes&nbsp;
in its coffin until they can climb out.

00:08:28.192 --> 00:08:32.560
And then, before they get ready&nbsp;
to hunt, they do have to poop.

00:08:36.584 --> 00:08:39.900
When Cienkowski decided to give&nbsp;
the vampyrellids their name,

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:42.926
did he imagine taking the metaphor this far?

00:08:42.926 --> 00:08:45.505
We don’t know, but we hope so.&nbsp;

00:08:45.505 --> 00:08:47.220
We hope that somewhere out there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:51.720
are the ghosts of a 19th century&nbsp;
conversation between biologists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:57.907
perhaps arguing the merits of comparing&nbsp;
these tiny organisms to a legendary figure.

00:08:57.907 --> 00:09:03.480
And perhaps they settle on understanding&nbsp;
that sometimes the only way to describe the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:09.806
fantastical worlds we see is to turn to&nbsp;
the fantastical creatures we imagine.

00:09:09.806 --> 00:09:14.248
And to wonder what other monsters we can find.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:26.037
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:09:26.037 --> 00:09:29.760
The people on the screen right&nbsp;
now, they are our Patreon patrons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:35.492
who allow us to dive deeper and deeper into&nbsp;
our really wonderful and fascinating world.

00:09:35.492 --> 00:09:39.188
A world that we all get to 
occupy together even if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.188 --> 00:09:43.844
don't know all the organisms&nbsp;
we are occupying it with.

00:09:43.844 --> 00:09:44.932
But that's a shame.

00:09:44.932 --> 00:09:47.880
We should know about them,&nbsp;
and that's why we're here.

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:49.920
So if you want to help us make this stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:56.120
you can go to Patreon.com/JourneytoMicro and have&nbsp;
your name be one of the names on this screen.

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:58.680
If you want to see more from our&nbsp;
Master of Microscopes, James Weiss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.214
you can check out Jam &amp; Germs on Instagram.

00:10:01.214 --> 00:10:05.940
And if you want to see more from us, I bet you&nbsp;
there's a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

